Z||000001||Gokart||01-07-2000||07:12 AM||||I've been looking for a Hotwheels "Fastback" GT for months now! All I see are 2M4's and 85 GT's. I betcha there rare, if they exsist at all?||||reg||4
Z||000002||ChadMan||01-07-2000||09:55 AM||||There is no Hot Wheels fastback Fiero, but ERTL made a beautiful diecast 1/43 scale GT in Medium Red Metallic.  It was sold as an 88, but it had silver wheels and gray "ground effects" so it was clearly an 87.  Expect to pay $30 or more for one of these.<P>I'll admit it - I collect Fiero toys and models.  I'll have to go home and count 'em.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||01-07-2000||12:55 PM||||I have several of the Hot Wheels and Matchbox Fieros, but Im just not willing to drop $30 on one of those model kits....or any of the other higher priced toys.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||01-07-2000||08:27 PM||||GoKart--What's wrong with '85 GTs?  They are absolutely the coolest looking Fiero out there!  Of course, that's just my opinion, but I AM KING!!  My name is Dave and I'm a paranoid Fieroholic with delusions of grandeur.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-07-2000||08:31 PM||||Gentlemen,<P>First off, the DGP racers were absolutely hands down the best looking Fiero's in relatively factory trim.  Secondly, MB made several variations on the paint scheme.  Sorry fastback whores.  If you are offended by that remark, I coined that term about myself.  While growing up I had an obsession with anycar that came in fastback trim.  Ferraris, Lotus Esprit, Fiero GT's you get the point.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000007||Gokart||01-07-2000||10:27 PM||||My first Fiero was a notchback and yes, they are the most beautiful cars ever made. Only 2nd to a "FASTBACK"! But we already had our preferences on another topic last month. So Chadman, what does ERTL stand for and do they still make those models or do I need to keep checking e-bay?||||reg||14
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-08-2000||06:59 AM||||GoKart--I doubt if anyone still makes models of Fieros. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Check your local hobby shop and see if they can find one for you.  Most of the time they have connections to other stores inventory just like car dealers.  Maybe another store has one sitting on a shelf collecting dust?||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||02:30 AM||||Everytime that I go to any store, I look to make sure that a Fiero model hasn't sat on the back of the shelf for all of these years... my search has been fruitless so far.<P>You can get most of your Fiero collectables from Ray Paulk.  He runs the Fiero Toy Box.  He is a great guy, but don't fill out the order form on his website.  You have to e-mail him personally.  I can get his e-mail for anybody that wants it.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000010||ChadMan||01-10-2000||11:56 AM||||Gokart, I'm not sure what ERTL stands for, but they are part of the AMT/ERTL toy company (AMT makes plastic models, ERTL makes diecasts).  They no longer make the little fastback Fiero, so you'll have to keep watching eBay.<P>ERTL still makes some cool diecast cars, which you can see at <A HREF="http://www.ertltoys.com." TARGET=_blank>www.ertltoys.com.</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Deer_Slayer||01-10-2000||03:06 PM||||I don't think they make a plactic model od and type of fiero now if ther ever did.  I checked the shelf of a local hobby shop and looked in the catalogues.  Saw none in any of those places.||||reg||9
Z||000012||ChadMan||01-10-2000||03:15 PM||||Actually, there were two basic types of Fiero models I know of:  Monogram and MPC.  Monogram made an 85 GT, while MPC made three variations of the same kit:  an 84 Coupe, an 85-86 Coupe (same as 84 but included "humped" decklid and incorrect side scoops) and an 86-87 GT (84 Coupe revised with fastback and correct side scoops, but incorrect decklid spoiler).  All the MPC Coupes included a carbureted 2.8 V6 and DGP bodywork as an options; the 86-87 GT came with the correct MPFI 2.8 but still retained the 2.5 Iron Duke as an option.  The MPC fastback kit was also sold as an 88, but retained the 86-87 suspension, so it's really not an 88.<P>Using these kits, it's possible to build a correct representation of any Fiero except an 87 Coupe or any 88.  I have built the following:<P>84 Coupe, red/gray, fully plumbed (plug wires, coolant tubes, brake lines, shift cables, PCV line, throttle cable)<BR>84 IMSA, yellow/green, partially plumbed, SD-4 with Webers (only half-a$$ accurate)<BR>85 GT, black/two-tone gray, partially plumbed<BR>87 GT, blue/two-tone gray, partially plumbed<BR>86 GT (w/88 paint scheme), red/tan, not plumbed<P>If you want to relive your childhood modeling days or build an accurate representation of your Fiero, I highly recommend any of these kits.  They're always available on eBay.  The Monogram is a little more realistic, but the MPC's offer more detail, including opening front and rear compartments, optional popped-up headlights, etc.<P>If you guys have any interest, I could possibly have my dad shoot some photos of my models and post them here.||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||01-12-2000||12:14 AM||||Chadman,<P>Shoot away, I am interested!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000014||DXR_DAD||01-12-2000||01:16 AM||||Thanks Chadman, please post the pics. I would like to have 1 model of my car||||reg||14
Z||000015||ChadMan||01-12-2000||09:13 AM||||It might be Monday or Tuesday before I get this done - my dad's got the nice photo equipment and I can't get over there til the weekend.<P>I'm working on an MPC 85 coupe that I'M convertible-izing right now.  See, my wife and I are trying to save for a house, which means I feel guilty spending money on anything more than maintenance for my real Fiero.  So I compensate by building models.||||reg||9
